# Competency: the deliberate-non-assertion report — every intentional boundary the
# ontology commits to must be enumerable, not merely respected in passing. A deliberate
# non-assertion is a logic:FormalizationCandidate flagged logic:candidateDeliberateNonAssertion
# true: an intentional refusal to over-assert that the foundation is held against. The flag
# deliberately cuts across logic:candidateCategory — a boundary may be a
# logic:CategoryNonEntailmentObligation (a conclusion the foundation must never draw, carried
# by a standing logic:NonEntailmentObligation and enforced by the Arm A / Arm B discharge
# check), a logic:CategoryIntegrityConstraint (a discipline whose logic:violation is a finding,
# e.g. frame-relativity or no-over-typing), or a logic:CategoryDeliberateOverlap (a mereological
# sharing fact deliberately admitted rather than disjointness-forbidden). Selecting on the flag
# (not the category) is what makes the WHOLE set queryable: the two integrity-constraint
# boundaries share their category with ordinary integrity constraints and cannot be told apart
# by category alone. Each row is one boundary with its kind; its rationale is the candidate's
# own skos:definition and logic:candidateScope, and — for the non-entailment ones — the
# forbidden predicate named by its linked obligation. Read over the production ontology (no
# synthetic data file) this answers "which deliberate non-assertions does the ontology
# guarantee, and of what kind?".
PREFIX logic: <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/logic/>

SELECT ?candidate ?category WHERE {
    ?candidate a logic:FormalizationCandidate ;
               logic:candidateCategory ?category ;
               logic:candidateDeliberateNonAssertion true .
}
ORDER BY ?candidate
